--TEST--
a `#[Test]` method is a row the runner reports, in declaration order, judged off the ledger (`rule:testing/test-attribute`, `rule:testing/failure-ledger`, `rule:testing/runner-is-strict`, `rule:testing/report-formats` and `rule:testing/nvst-is-separate`)
--RUN--
test
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: lang:errors/assertion-failures, lang:testing/a-test-is-a-method-marked-test
// § 23: `nvs test` on a program runs its `#[Test]` methods, so this case is
// the runner's own report rather than the program's output. `--RUN--` above
// is what says so; every other case in this tree is `nvs run`.
use Core\Test;

// § 20: class order is the roster's sorted order, so `AlphaTest` is reported
// before `ZebraTest` however the two are written here.
final class ZebraTest {
    #[Test]
    public function zulu(): void {
        Test::assertSame("z", "z");
    }
}

final class AlphaTest {
    // § 1: within a class the order is declaration order, which is what a
    // reader of one file sees — these three names sort the other way round.
    #[Test]
    public function zeroIsWrittenFirst(): void {
        Test::assertSame(0, 0);
    }

    #[Test]
    public function oneIsWrittenSecond(): void {
        Test::assertSame(1, 1);
    }

    // § 20: a skip states a reason, and its test is never constructed — the
    // assertion below would fail if it were.
    #[Test(skip: "the behaviour it pins is not written yet")]
    public function twoIsSkippedWithItsReason(): void {
        Test::assertSame(2, 3);
    }

    // § 4: a failure names both sides by name, so a reversed call still reads
    // correctly.
    #[Test]
    public function aFailedAssertionNamesBothSides(): void {
        Test::assertSame(2 + 2, 5);
    }

    // § 5: the verdict is read off the ledger and not off the exception state,
    // so a body that caught its own failure has still failed.
    #[Test]
    public function aCaughtFailureStillFails(): void {
        try {
            Test::assertSame("actual", "expected");
        } catch (Core\Test\Failure $swallowed) {
            Test::assertSame("caught", "caught");
        }
    }

    // § 20: a test that asserts nothing fails, which is what an empty ledger
    // means.
    #[Test]
    public function aTestThatAssertsNothingFails(): void {
    }
}
--EXPECTF--
  AlphaTest
    ✓ zeroIsWrittenFirst               %f ms
    ✓ oneIsWrittenSecond               %f ms
    - twoIsSkippedWithItsReason        %f ms
      skipped: the behaviour it pins is not written yet
    ✗ aFailedAssertionNamesBothSides   %f ms
      Core\Test::assertSame failed: `$actual` is 4, `$expected` is 5
    ✗ aCaughtFailureStillFails         %f ms
      Core\Test::assertSame failed: `$actual` is "actual", `$expected` is "expected"
    ✗ aTestThatAssertsNothingFails     %f ms
      it asserted nothing: `rule:testing/runner-is-strict` fails a test whose ledger is empty — write `Core\Test::assertDoesNotThrow` where the claim is that a call completes
  ZebraTest
    ✓ zulu                             %f ms

  3 failed, 3 passed, 1 skipped, 0 flaky in %d ms
--EXPECTF-ERROR--
